## Performance Profiling Guide

### When to Profile

- App frame rate drops below 60 fps (stutters).
- Example takes >100ms to render a single frame.
- WASM build is slower than native.
- Scrolling lists with >100 items causes lag.

### Where to Look

| Component | Metric | Measure With |
|-----------|--------|--------------|
| View function | Time to rebuild the `El<S>` tree | Add timing in your view function: `let t0 = std::time::Instant::now(); let el = view(app); println!("view: {:?}", t0.elapsed());` |
| Layout | Time to measure and arrange elements | Add timing around `layout()` calls in `shell/mod.rs` (line 250). |
| Paint | Time to paint elements to pixels | Add timing around `paint()` in `shell/mod.rs` (line 260). |
| Canvas | Time to allocate and rasterise pixels | Check `canvas.rs` line 1; measure allocation and fill time. |
| Text | Time to rasterise glyphs | Add timing around `text()` calls; check `text.rs` line 200 for glyph cache. |
| Platform | Time blocked waiting for OS events | Check platform backend `pump()` calls; they're blocking. |

### Optimization Checklist

**In the view function:**
- ❌ Create large vectors or collect entire lists: `items.iter().collect::<Vec<_>>()` allocates every frame.
- ✅ Use iterators: `items.iter().map(…).collect()` is fine; avoid multiple passes.
- ❌ Call `.clone()` on large data structures.
- ✅ Pass `&item` references into the view tree.
- ❌ Do layout calculations in the view function.
- ✅ Let the layout engine handle measurement.

**In handlers:**
- ❌ Block on I/O (network, disk) in a handler.
- ✅ Return immediately and update state; let the next frame handle I/O results.

**In custom paint:**
- ❌ Draw millions of small shapes per frame (`for i in 0..1_000_000 { painter.fill(...) }`).
- ✅ Draw in batches or use pre-computed surfaces.
- ❌ Create text for every character manually.
- ✅ Use the `text()` widget which batches glyph rasterising.

**For lists:**
- ❌ Rebuild all list items when one changes. Every reorder redraws all text glyphs.
- ✅ Use `.key(&item.id)` on list items. Unchanged items preserve hover/focus state; only changed ones re-render.

**For WASM:**
- ❌ Use `console.log()` inside the frame loop; it's slow.
- ✅ Log only on state changes or in handlers.
- ❌ Measure performance in the browser console; GC pauses skew the result.
- ✅ Use DevTools Performance tab; record profiles to see the real frame time.

### Profiling Tools

**Rust (native):**
```bash
# Build with optimizations
cargo build --release

# Use a profiler (macOS example)
instruments -t "System Trace" ./target/release/rui_example

# Or inline timing
time cargo run -p rui --example counter
```

**JavaScript (WASM):**
```javascript
// In the browser console
const start = performance.now();
listen_counter();
present_counter();
console.log(`Frame: ${(performance.now() - start).toFixed(2)}ms`);
```

**Synthetic load testing:**
```rust
// In tests/integration.rs, create an app with large state
let app = App { items: (0..10_000).collect() };
let t0 = std::time::Instant::now();
let frame = view(&app);
println!("Large frame: {:?}", t0.elapsed());
```

### Red Flags

| Signal | Problem | Solution |
|--------|---------|----------|
| View function takes >10ms | Too much allocation or computation | Profile; reduce `.clone()` and iteration |
| Layout takes >5ms | Too many elements or deeply nested | Simplify layout; use `layer()` for overlays instead of nesting |
| Paint takes >20ms | Too many shapes being drawn | Batch drawing; pre-compute surfaces where possible |
| WASM slower than native by >2x | JavaScript overhead or unoptimized WASM | Check if you're using `console.log()` in the loop; measure with DevTools |
| Memory grows unbounded | Leak in `Memory` or canvas buffer | Check that old elements are being garbage collected; verify `.key()` usage |

---

## Common Mistakes & How to Avoid Them

| Mistake | Symptom | Fix |
|---------|---------|-----|
| Capturing state in a closure instead of receiving it as an argument | `error: cannot borrow app as mutable` in handler | Change `let app = ...` to receiving `\|app: &mut App\|` as a handler parameter |
| Rebuilding state inside the view function | View function has side effects; state changes unpredictably | Move side effects out of the view function; state changes only in handlers |
| Not using `.key()` on reordered lists | Hover/focus state "sticks" to the wrong element when list reorders | Add `.key(&item.id)` to each element in a dynamically reordered list |
| Using `.clone()` everywhere | Code is hard to follow; performance suffers | Use references (`&item`) and let the view function own the data |
| Hardcoding colors instead of using `Tone` | App breaks in dark mode; WASM doesn't match native | Always use `Tone::Accent`, `Tone::Surface`, etc.; never hardcode RGB |
| Testing only the happy path | Edge cases break in production | Test: empty state, boundary values (0, max, negative), invalid input |
| Committing without running tests | CI fails; work blocks the team | Always run `cargo test --lib` and `cargo test --test recipes` before commit |

---

## API Surface Reference

These are the types and functions you'll use most when working on rui:

### Core Types

| Type | Module | Use |
|------|--------|-----|
| `El<S>` | `element` | The UI element tree. Everything is an `El<S>`. |
| `Event` | `input` | Platform events: `Click(point)`, `Key(key)`, `Wheel(delta)`, `Drag(…)`. |
| `Painter<'_>` | `paint` | Draw API: `fill()`, `stroke()`, `text()`, `image()`. |
| `Canvas` | `canvas` | Pixel buffer; `Canvas::new()` creates a blank canvas for tests. |
| `Rect` | `geom` | A rectangle: `x, y, w, h`. Used everywhere for layout. |
| `Tone` | `theme` | Color roles: `Accent`, `Surface`, `Muted`, `Success`, `Warning`, `Error`. Never hardcode RGB. |
| `Appearance` | `theme` | Light or dark mode; detected from OS or overridden in tests. |
| `Length` | `style` | Sizing: `Auto`, `Fixed(pixels)`, `Grow(flex_weight)`. |
| `Harness<S>` | `testing` | Test driver. `Harness::new(state, view)` runs a frame with no window. |

### Common Functions

| Function | Module | Use |
|----------|--------|-----|
| `App::run(state, view)` | `app` | Start the app; blocks until window closes. |
| `col(elements)`, `row(elements)` | `element` | Stack elements vertically or horizontally. |
| `draw(size, \|painter, rect\| { … })` | `widgets` | Paint custom shapes; called on every frame. |
| `button(label, handler)` | `widgets` | Clickable button. Handler: `\|app: &mut S\| { … }`. |
| `text(string)` | `widgets` | Render text; inherits size and color from parent. |
| `text_size(size, element)` | `widgets` | Set text size for this element and children. |
| `fill(color, element)` | `element` | Set background color. |
| `gap(pixels, elements)` | `element` | Add space between items. |
| `.on_click(handler)` | `element` | Attach a click handler. Handler called after frame. |
| `.on_drag(handler)` | `element` | Attach a drag handler. Receives `Drag { from, to, … }`. |
| `.on_key(handler)` | `element` | Attach a keyboard handler. Receives `Key` and modifier flags. |
| `.key(id)` | `element` | Preserve element identity for state (hover, focus, scroll) across reorders. |
| `Harness::click_text(label)` | `testing` | Click the first element containing text (in a test). |
| `Harness::click(point)` | `testing` | Click at pixel (x, y). |
| `Harness::frame().shows(text)` | `testing` | Assert text is visible in the rendered frame. |

### Patterns You'll Repeat

```rust
// Define state
struct App { counter: i32 }

// Define view (rebuilds every frame)
fn view(app: &App) -> El<App> {
    col((
        text(format!("Count: {}", app.counter)),
        button("Increment", |app: &mut App| app.counter += 1),
    ))
}

// Run the app
App::run(App { counter: 0 }, view);
```

```rust
// Test the app (no window needed)
#[test]
fn counter_increments_on_click() {
    let mut harness = Harness::new(App { counter: 0 }, view);
    harness.click_text("Increment");
    assert_eq!(harness.state().counter, 1);
}
```

---

## Git Workflow

- Pre-commit hook: Runs `cargo fmt --check` and `cargo clippy`. Bypass only in emergencies with `--no-verify`.
- Commits: Prefix with platform or feature (e.g., "Add X11 input handling", "Improve text rendering").
- Tests: Always run `cargo test --lib` and `cargo test --test recipes` before pushing.

---

## Developer Glossary

**Backend** — Platform-specific window and event handler. Implements six methods: `open()`, `pump()`, `surface()`, `appearance()`, `present()`, `is_open()`. One per OS (macOS, Windows, X11, WASM). The shell event loop calls only these methods; everything above is platform-agnostic.

**Canvas** — In-memory pixel buffer. Holds the current frame as RGBA pixels. `Canvas::draw()` is the root drawing operation. Tests create a canvas with no window via `Harness::new()`.

**Element** (`El<S>`) — A UI element tree node. Describes structure, style, and behavior. Immutable; rebuilt every frame from state. Builders: `col()`, `row()`, `.on_click()`, `.fill()`, `.pad()`, etc.

**Frame** — One rendered image. The entire cycle: collect input → call view → layout → paint → present to screen. Happens 60 times per second; every frame rebuilds the element tree from state.

**Handler** — A function that mutates state in response to user input. Signature: `|app: &mut S| { … }`. Handlers receive mutable state as an argument, not closures. Called *after* the frame is rendered, so no re-entrancy.

**Harness** — Test driver that renders a frame to a buffer without a window. `Harness::new(state, view)` creates a testable app. Methods: `click_text()`, `drag()`, `frame()`, `save_png()`. Used in `tests/recipes.rs`.

**Identity** — An element's position in the tree. Preserved across frame rebuilds. Used to maintain hover/focus/scroll state. Can be explicit via `.key(&id)` or implicit from tree position.

**Input** — The module that translates raw `Event` stream (platform events) into per-frame immediate-mode queries. `Input::on_frame()` is called once per frame to process accumulated events.

**Layout Engine** — Flexbox-like algorithm in `layout.rs`. Measures elements (`Size`), then arranges them to a container (`Rect`). Supports `Length::Auto`, `Fixed(pixels)`, `Grow(weight)` for sizing; `Align` and `Justify` for positioning.

**Memory** — Stateful interaction data: hover state, focus, scroll position, text caret, animation state. Keyed by element identity (path in tree or explicit `.key()`). Persists across frame rebuilds if identity is preserved.

**Painter** — The drawing API. Methods: `fill(rect, radius, tone)`, `stroke(rect, radius, width, tone)`, `text(rect, string, size, color)`. Always used inside `draw()` primitives. Colors come from theme (never hardcoded RGB).

**Role** (Tone) — Semantic color names: `Accent`, `Surface`, `Muted`, `Success`, `Warning`, `Error`. Mapped to actual RGB by the theme, respecting light/dark mode. Use roles, not hardcoded colors.

**State** — Application data (a Rust struct). Immutable during frame rendering. Changed only by handlers. The entire UI rebuilds from state every frame (view is a pure function of state).

**Synthetic Font** — A test font with predictable widths. Each character is 0.5 em wide (half an em), so text width = string length × 0.5 em. Ensures tests are deterministic (no platform font variations).

**Tone** — Semantic color role. See `Role`.

**Turn** — One frame iteration. The `turn()` function in `shell/mod.rs` (lines 325–358) is the frame-stepping abstraction: collect input → call view → layout → paint → check pixels → animate. Called once per frame on all platforms (60 fps on desktop, `requestAnimationFrame` on WASM).

**View** — A pure function that returns an `El<S>` from application state. Signature: `fn view(app: &App) -> El<App> { … }`. Called every frame; rebuilds the entire UI description. No side effects.

**Visual State** — Transient appearance state: hover (mouse over), focus (has keyboard focus), held (button pressed), disabled (not clickable). Tracked by `Visual` in `paint.rs`. Flows from input to paint without being stored in app state.

---

## Code Patterns You'll See in the Codebase

Understanding these patterns helps you read and modify code confidently:

### Pattern 1: Builder Pattern for Elements

**Where:** `src/element.rs`, `src/widgets.rs`

**What it looks like:**
```rust
col((
    text("Hello"),
    button("Click me", |app: &mut App| app.count += 1),
))
.pad(10.0)
.gap(5.0)
.fill(Tone::Surface)
```

**Why:** Fluent API lets you describe UI structure, style, and behavior in one expression. Each method returns `self` (or a transformed `self`) to enable chaining.

### Pattern 2: Generic Handler Closures

**Where:** `src/widgets.rs` (any widget with `.on_click()`, `.on_drag()`, `.on_key()`)

**What it looks like:**
```rust
pub fn button<S>(label: &str, on_click: impl Fn(&mut S) + Copy + 'static) -> El<S> {
    // ...
}

// Usage
button("Increment", |app: &mut App| app.counter += 1)
```

**Why:** Handlers receive `&mut S` (mutable state) as an argument, not captured via closure. This enables state mutation without `Rc<RefCell<>>`. The `+ Copy + 'static` bounds ensure the handler can be stored in the element tree and called multiple times.

### Pattern 3: Keyed Lists for Identity Preservation

**Where:** `src/memory.rs`, `src/element.rs::.key()`

**What it looks like:**
```rust
col(
    items.iter().map(|item| {
        text(&item.name)
            .key(&item.id)  // ← Preserve identity across reorders
    }).collect::<Vec<_>>()
)
```

**Why:** Without `.key()`, an element's position in the tree is its identity. If the list reorders, hover/focus/scroll state follows the wrong element. `.key()` pins identity to a stable value so state sticks to the right element even if it moves in the tree.

### Pattern 4: Conditional Rendering

**Where:** `src/widgets.rs`, `examples/controls.rs`

**What it looks like:**
```rust
col((
    text("Status:"),
    if app.loading {
        text("Loading...")
    } else {
        button("Click", |app: &mut App| app.do_thing())
    },
))
```

**Why:** Rust's `if` expression returns a value. Both branches must have the same type, so you often use `.into()` to coerce `El<AppA>` and `El<AppB>` to a common container type. Alternatively, wrap in a common element type (`col`, `row`, etc.).

### Pattern 5: Painter Calls Inside `draw()`

**Where:** `src/widgets.rs`, `tests/recipes.rs`

**What it looks like:**
```rust
draw(Size::new(100.0, 20.0), |painter, rect| {
    // Fill background
    painter.fill(rect, Radius::Pill, Tone::Surface);
    // Stroke border
    painter.stroke(rect, Radius::Pill, 2.0, Tone::Muted);
    // Draw text
    painter.text(rect, "Label", 12.0, Tone::Text);
})
```

**Why:** `draw()` is how you paint custom shapes. `Painter` provides `fill()`, `stroke()`, and `text()` methods. Always use `Tone` (semantic roles), never hardcoded colors.

### Pattern 6: Iterator Chains in Views

**Where:** Any view with dynamic lists

**What it looks like:**
```rust
col(
    app.items.iter()
        .enumerate()
        .map(|(i, item)| {
            button(&item.name, move |app: &mut App| app.select(i))
                .key(&item.id)
        })
        .collect::<Vec<_>>()
)
```

**Why:** Views build element trees from data. Use iterators to map domain data to UI elements. Collect to `Vec<El<S>>` at the end. The `move` keyword captures `i` for the handler closure.

### Pattern 7: Theme Colors (Tone, Not RGB)

**Where:** Everywhere colors appear

**What it looks like:**
```rust
// ✗ Wrong
painter.fill(rect, Radius::None, Color::from_rgba(255, 0, 0, 255)); // Red (hardcoded)

// ✓ Right
painter.fill(rect, Radius::None, Tone::Error); // Maps to theme color
```

**Why:** Tones are semantic roles that respect light/dark mode. Hardcoding RGB breaks dark mode and makes WASM parity testing harder. Always use `Tone` from the current theme.

### Pattern 8: Testing with Harness

**Where:** `tests/recipes.rs`, any new test file

**What it looks like:**
```rust
#[test]
fn my_feature_works() {
    let mut harness = Harness::new(App { state: 0 }, view);

    // Interact
    harness.click_text("Button");

    // Assert
    assert_eq!(harness.state().state, 1);
    assert!(harness.frame().shows("updated"));
}
```

**Why:** `Harness` drives the frame logic without a window, using a deterministic synthetic font. Fast, repeatable, no platform dependencies.

### Pattern 9: Platform-Specific Code (Backend Trait)

**Where:** `src/shell/mod.rs`, `src/shell/platform/*.rs`

**What it looks like:**
```rust
trait Backend: Sized {
    fn open(options: &WindowOptions) -> Result<Self, Error>;
    fn pump(&mut self, timeout: Duration, events: &mut Vec<Event>, …) -> Result<(), Error>;
    fn surface(&self) -> (u32, u32, f32); // width, height, scale
    fn appearance(&self) -> Appearance;   // light or dark
    fn present(&self, canvas: &Canvas) -> Result<(), Error>;
    fn is_open(&self) -> bool;
}

// Implemented once per OS in src/shell/platform/macos.rs, windows.rs, etc.
```

**Why:** Platform code is isolated behind a six-method interface. Everything above that line is shared. Adding a new platform means implementing these six methods, nothing more.

### Pattern 10: Element Identity and Key

**Where:** `src/element.rs`, `src/memory.rs`

**What it looks like:**
```rust
// Identity comes from tree position by default
col((
    text("First"),   // Position 0 → identity 0
    text("Second"),  // Position 1 → identity 1
))

// Explicit identity for reordered lists
col(
    items.iter().map(|item| {
        text(&item.name).key(&item.id)  // ← Explicit identity
    }).collect::<Vec<_>>()
)
```

**Why:** Memory (hover, focus, scroll, animations) is keyed by element identity. Without `.key()`, identity is positional; reordering breaks state. With `.key()`, identity is explicit and stable.

